sophia: FYI - onboarding sample app (notes-example) is failing its test matrix in CI. Both vitest and jest fail but different errors. Looks ESM/CJS related again.

dev-bot: nightly-notes-example  build/notes-example #729 failed 
```
Error: Cannot use import statement outside a module
    at Object.compileFunction (node:vm:360:18)
    at Module._compile (internal/modules/cjs/loader:1092:27)
```

lee: repro locally with `pnpm -w test --filter notes-example -- run unit`. Vitest resolves `@redwood/sdk/runtime` to the mjs entry (import), while jest (transformIgnorePatterns) ends up loading the cjs. Our fetch+kv mocks attach to different module objects so tests don't see the mock.

ana: (typo: i'm ana) We have conditional exports in SDK package.json: 
```
"exports": {
  "./runtime": { "import": "./dist/runtime.mjs", "require": "./dist/runtime.cjs" }
}
```
That means node/runners pick different files. Vitest's Vite resolver follows the import field. Jest's resolver + transformIgnorePatterns might load the cjs.

jordan: Also ran into types trouble: TS can't pick up types for the mjs runtime in the sample app unless we publish a `types` entry or `exports.types`. Some consumers import `@redwood/sdk/runtime` and get `any`. That makes tests w/ ts-jest noisier.

sophia: Proposed quick fix we used in infra: (1) add a tiny require-shim for tests, (2) force vitest to inline the sdk, (3) map jest to the shim. Worked across linux/mac.

lee: snippets pasted: vitest.config.ts tweak:
```ts
export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    deps: { inline: ['@redwood/sdk'] },
    setupFiles: ['./tests/setup/vitest-global.ts']
  }
})
```
jest.config.js tweak:
```js
module.exports = {
  transformIgnorePatterns: ['node_modules/(?!@redwood/sdk)'],
  moduleNameMapper: { '^@redwood/sdk/runtime$': '<rootDir>/tests/setup/require-shim.cjs' }
}
```
shim (tests/setup/require-shim.cjs):
```js
const { createRequire } = require('module');
const req = createRequire(import.meta.url || __filename);
module.exports = req('../../dist/runtime.cjs');
```
vitest setup (tests/setup/vitest-global.ts):
```ts
import runtime from '../../dist/runtime.mjs';
// attach fetch mock to runtime
(globalThis as any).fetch = runtime.fetch;
```

ana: Another option: publish an `exports` variant for testing only (e.g., `exports: { './runtime': { 'test': './dist/runtime.cjs', 'import': './dist/runtime.mjs' } }`) and enable via NODE_OPTIONS in CI. But that's messy. Shim + inline is lower risk.

jordan: For types: add "types": "dist/runtime.d.ts" at package root and add an `exports.types` mapping so TS picks definitions regardless of import path. That prevented a bunch of ts-jest headaches in the sample app.

lee: One more gotcha: when running vitest in ESM node mode, `createRequire(import.meta.url)` inside the shim fails unless the shim is CJS (we made it .cjs). So ensure shim filenames end with .cjs and are referenced from jest moduleNameMapper.

sophia: I opened PR https://git.internal/redwood/sdk/pull/812 which: adds the require-shim, updates vitest/jest configs in the sample app, and publishes `types` + `exports.types`. CI green on linux/mac; windows runner still has a flaky path resolution failing on long path names.

ana: For docs/devex: we should add a short page "Testing ESM packages with Jest & Vitest" describing: conditional exports pitfalls, vitest deps.inline, jest moduleNameMapper + transformIgnorePatterns, require-shim pattern, and types/export tips. I'll draft and link the PR.

jordan: TL;DR for folks hitting this: if you see different module identities between runners, try the require-shim + deps.inline combo first. If ts types are missing, add `types`/`exports.types` to package.json.

sophia: thanks, team. pushing the PR merge to staging. Reopen if anything new pops up. :white_check_mark:
